CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ MD->MS construction preserves value: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
[temp.deduct]/5 with [temp.constr.decl]/1: a function-template specialisation
whose associated constraints (its requires-clause) are not satisfied by the
deduced arguments is removed from the candidate set.

Mutually-convertible class types that each have a templated integral
constructor `C(T) requires integral<T>` and an explicit converting constructor
taking the other type (a minimal model of libstdc++'s
std::ranges::__detail::__max_size_type / __max_diff_type).  Constructing MS from
an MD -- as __to_unsigned_like does via __max_size_type(__t) -- previously
recursed without bound and crashed (SIGSEGV): overload resolution considered the
templated constructor with T deduced as the *other class type* (integral<MD> is
false, but the requires-clause was not checked for a templated constructor),
producing a bogus by-value constructor whose argument had to be materialised by
constructing the same type again, ad infinitum.

guess_function_template_args now evaluates a deduced candidate's requires-clause
(in addition to the existing parameter-level #C_concept_constraint check) and
discards the candidate when the constraint is unsatisfied, breaking the
recursion.
